1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The whole world knows — these
three stars.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:10,000
China calls them Shen. Egypt: Sah.
Australia’s Kamilaroi: Birray Birray.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Three near-equal stars in a straight
line, under 3° long.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Almost on the celestial equator, they
rise due east everywhere — from the
Arctic to New Zealand.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:31,000
The Kamilaroi’s Birray Birray are three
young boys: the Belt. Rigel is their
campfire; the Sword, their fire stick.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The Maya: the first fire of creation.
Three stars are its hearthstones; the
Orion Nebula, its smoke.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:48,000
The Aztecs saw a fire drill. Every 52
years, all fires were put out and
priests drilled a new one on a hilltop.

8
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:56,000
If no fire came, the sun would not rise
again.

9
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Red Betelgeuse upper left, blue-white
Rigel lower right, the Belt between.
Below it, the brightest star: Sirius.

10
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Navajo: star stories told only in
winter.

11
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

